// Each is a powerful concept in SenseTalk worth understanding to make your scripts more readable
// Add some break points to step through code and see how SenseTalk is working

(* Section 1: Introduction to Each *)
(* This section introduces the concept of "each" in SenseTalk and demonstrates its basic usage. *)
put "How big is each and how does it process a chunk of data?"
// An "each" expression works with any chunk type and results in a list
put each character of "Elephant"
put each item of "red,orange,yellow"
put each word of "Hamlet"
put each item of [3,5,7,9]

put - // used to divide the sections of the script

(* Section 2: Filtering with Each *)
(* This section shows how to use "where", "which", and "whose" clauses with "each" to filter items based on conditions. *)
put "Can I use a where clause with each?"
// A Where clause lets you select certain items
put each character of "William Shakespeare" where each is in "aeiou"
put each word of "Hamlet" where the length of each is more than 3
put each item of [3,5,7,9,11] where each is a multiple of 3

put "Can I use a which instead of where with each? Yes!"
// "Which" can enhance readability and feels more natural
// It takes the place of "where each" or "each"
put each item of 1..100 where each is a multiple of 17
put each item of 1..100 which is a multiple of 17
put each item of 1..100 which is a multiple of 17 and which is an even number

put "Can I use a whose instead of which or where with each? Yes! Remember whose is possessive"
// "Whose" is similar but includes the possessive: where each's
set quote to {{
    It's not that I'm so smart, it's just that I stay with problems longer.
    - Albert Einstein
}}
put each word of quote where each's length is more than 4
put each word of quote whose length is more than 4 and which contains "s"

put - // used to divide the sections of the script

(* Section 3: Performing Operations with Each *)
(* This section shows how to perform operations on each item in a collection. *)
put "How can I perform an operation with each item and do the action on all items?"
put each item of 1..10 squared
put 100 plus each item of 1..10
put "M" & each character of "aeiou"
put each word of "Hamlet"
put the length of each word of "Hamlet"

(* Section 4: Using Operators with Where Clause *)
(* This section demonstrates the use of operators along with a "where" clause in "each" expressions. *)
put "Using operators along with a Where clause"
put each item of 1..10 squared
put each item of 1..10 where each is a multiple of 2
put each item of 1..10 where each is a multiple of 2 squared
put each item of 1..10 {where each is a multiple of 2} squared

put - // used to divide the sections of the script

(* Section 5: Expressiveness of Each *)
(* This section highlights the expressiveness of "each" expressions without adding new functionality. *)
put "Each expressions add great expressiveness but no new functionality"
put each character of "eggplant" into eChars
put eChars
set eChars to an empty list
repeat with each character of "eggplant"
    insert it into eChars
end repeat
put eChars
put -
put the length of each word of "Hamlet" which contains "R" into rLengths
put rLengths
set rLengths to an empty list
repeat with each word of "Hamlet"
    if it contains "R" then
        insert the length of it into rLengths
    end if
end repeat
put rLengths

put - // used to divide the sections of the script

(* Section 6: Using Of/For Each *)
(* This section explains the additional flexibility provided by "of each" and "for each" for generating results. *)
put "Using 'for each' gives additional flexibility for generating results"
put each item of 1..10 {where each is a multiple of 3} squared
put each squared for each item of 1..10 where each is a multiple of 3
put the square root of each item of 1..10 rounded to 2 places
--put round(the square root of each item of 1..10 , 2)
put round(the square root of each , 2) for each item of 1..10
set numbers to 10..20
put each && "squared is" && each squared for each item of numbers into squareTexts
put squareTexts joined by return
set myText to "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog"
put word number of "the" within myText
put the counter for each word of myText which is "the"

put - // used to divide the sections of the script

(* Section 7: Containing Each Expressions *)
(* This section discusses the need to contain "each" expressions using parentheses to avoid issues with surrounding operators. *)
put "Because an Each expression expands to include surrounding operators, sometimes you'll need to contain it by enclosing it in parentheses"
put each word of "Hamlet" which contains "R"
put the number of items in each word of "Hamlet" which contains "R"
put the number of items in (each word of "Hamlet" which contains "R")
set numList to [4, -8, 17, -5, -2, 6, 12]
put the average of each item in numList which is greater than 0
put the average of (each item in numList which is greater than 0)

put - // used to divide the sections of the script

(* Section 8: Using Every Expression *)
(* This section introduces the "every" expression to test conditions across all items in a collection. *)
put "Use an Every expression to test whether something is true for every value in a collection "
set numbers to [3,42,12,96,102]
put each item of numbers is a multiple of 3 -- this doesn't quite work
if every item of numbers is a multiple of 3 then put "All Triples" else put "NOT!"
put every item of numbers {which is an even number} is greater than 10
// Variations include "not every", "at least one", "none" or "not one"
if not every item of numbers is an even number then put "Some are Odd"
if at least one item of numbers is an even number then put "Contains an Even number"
if none of the items of numbers is greater than 150 then put "Nothing over 150"
if not one word of "Hamlet" begins with "S" then put "No S Words"
if the length of at least one word of "Hamlet" > 4 then put "Long Words"

put "End of Each Demo"
